---
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Quinyte
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// PLACEHOLDER. This page exists to prove the layout, the tokens and the
// components render. It carries no product copy on purpose: the information
// architecture and the landing narrative belong to another agent, and
// inventing them here would collide with that work.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ClaimClass from "@assayd/web-shared/ClaimClass.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="assayd"
  description="Scaffold placeholder for the assayd site."
  current="/index.html"
>
  <h1>Placeholder</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This page proves the shell: tokens, type scale, navigation, and the shared
    components. It is not the landing page and carries no product copy.
  </p>

  {/*
    ONE CLAIM PER BLOCK. The inline badge is a <span>, so it cannot break the
    paragraph it sits in — which means a second badge in this same <p> would
    share its block. A retraction's rescue scope is the enclosing block, so two
    claims in one block means either can read as covered by the other's
    evidence: the exact failure the claim classes exist to prevent.

    `pnpm run check:claims` fails on it rather than trusting this comment. An
    earlier version of this paragraph invited the unsafe pattern in so many
    words, which is how it got found.
  */}
  <p>
    A claim can appear in a sentence — like this one <ClaimClass
      claim="designed"
      design="design 03"
      section="§3.3.2"
    /> — without breaking the line it sits in. It must be the only claim in
    that sentence's block; a second one belongs in its own paragraph, list item
    or header badge.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-top: var(--ax-space-3);
    font-size: var(--ax-body-lg);
    line-height: var(--ax-leading-snug);
    color: var(--ax-ink-soft);
  }

  p + p {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-top: var(--ax-space-4);
  }
</style>
